// @run
// @log true
// @log false
// @log false
// @log true
// @log false
// @log true
// @hint redundant type annotation: `a` is inferred as `{kids: {v: i32}[]}` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `b` is inferred as `{kids: {v: i32}[]}` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c` is inferred as `{kids: {v: i32}[]}` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `d` is inferred as `{kids: {v: i32}[]}` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: P` annotations are DECLARED redundant rather than removed: they are what makes each
// binding the same NOMINAL row, which is the shape this row is about. `e` and `f` keep theirs
// implicitly (an empty literal has nothing to infer from).
//
// D1017 — `==` over a struct whose FIELD is a list of structs. `vl check` returned 0 and emit
// refused with "unsupported struct field type in equality".
//
// THE ACCEPTANCE HERE IS THE VALUE TABLE, not the compile. On this same `==` ladder D989's
// half-wiring compiled cleanly and printed `1 == 2` as `true`, so equal / differing-element /
// differing-length / empty-vs-empty are all pinned below.
//
// Nothing new was built. A ref-list field compares exactly as a ref list does at top level:
// push the two lists, hand them to the shared core, whose struct-element class already routes
// to the stash-based `emitStructEqRec(body, -2, -3, …)` — the same machinery that made
// `L[] == L[]` run all along. The arm only had to READ the two lists off a field path instead
// of off two operands, and collection had to note the compare frame: `leqNoteBin` derives
// frames from the BINARY'S OWN OPERANDS, which for a struct equality are structs, so it never
// noted one for a list a field holds.
type L = { v: i32 }

type P = { kids: L[] }

const a: P = { kids: [{ v: 1 }, { v: 2 }] }

const b: P = { kids: [{ v: 1 }, { v: 2 }] }

const c: P = { kids: [{ v: 1 }, { v: 9 }] }

const d: P = { kids: [{ v: 1 }] }

const e: P = { kids: [] }

const f: P = { kids: [] }

print(a == b)

print(a == c)

print(a == d)

print(e == f)

print(a == e)

print(a != c)
